# {{.Name}}

A service built on the [rizotto]({{.RizottoModule}}) framework, generated by `rizotto make-project`.
The repository holds two parts: the go server in `server/` and its React app in `web/`.
The go module lives in `server/`, so its path is the repository followed by that
directory: `{{.Module}}`.

## Getting started

```sh
task deps        # resolve the go and the node dependencies
task migrate-up  # start PostgreSQL in docker and apply the migrations
task start       # run the server on $PORT (9090 by default)
task web:dev     # run the React app on :5173, /api is proxied to the server
task test        # run the server tests
task lint        # run golangci-lint
task gen         # regenerate the service bindings and the sqlc queries
task docker:build # build the docker image {{.Slug}}: the server with the web app
task docker:build:migrations # build the image {{.Slug}}-migrations that migrates a database
```

Docker is required: PostgreSQL, dbmate and sqlc all run in containers.

The root `Taskfile.yml` forwards to `server/Taskfile.yml` and `web/Taskfile.yml`;
inside either directory `task` runs that part alone.

Configuration is read from `server/.env`, which imports the defaults from `server/.env.example`.
Values already present in the environment win over both files.

If `task deps` cannot download `{{.RizottoModule}}`, either export
`GOPRIVATE={{.RizottoModule}}` or point the module at a local checkout:

```sh
cd server && go mod edit -replace {{.RizottoModule}}=../../rizotto
```

## How it works

```sh
rizotto skill              # what this project is made of
rizotto service skill      # services in detail
rizotto controller skill   # controllers in detail
rizotto repository skill   # queries, columns and migrations in detail
```

`.agents/skills/rizotto/SKILL.md` points coding agents at those same commands, so an
agent working in this repository asks the installed binary instead of guessing at
the conventions. `.agents/skills/database/SKILL.md` is the procedure for changing the
schema: which task touches which database, how a migration is written and what it
may do while the previous release is still running.

## Layout

    Taskfile.yml                         forwards to the Taskfiles of server/ and web/
    .agents/skills/rizotto/              the agent skill describing how to work in this project
    .agents/skills/database/             the agent skill describing how to change the database schema
    .docker/                             the Dockerfiles of the two images, their .dockerignore files and the .env the server ships

    server/                              the go module
    server/server.go                     wires the settings, the services and the HTTP routes
    server/sqlc.yaml                     one entry per repository: its queries, its schema and the generated package
    server/docker-compose.yml            the local PostgreSQL and the dbmate that migrates it
    server/scripts/migrate.sh            runs dbmate over the migrations of every service
    server/api/controller.go             HTTP contexts shared by the controllers (authentication lives here)
    server/api/doc/                      the OpenAPI document the route tests fill in
    server/services/item/                the item service: the RPC implementation
    server/services/item/item-client/    the item contract; bind-gen.go is generated from client.go by genbind
    server/services/item/repository/     database access: sqlc queries, generated db package and dbmate migrations

    web/                                 the React app (Vite + TypeScript)
    web/src/                             its sources; web/dist is the build

The sample `item` service is a starting point: rename it, or add the next service
with the command below.

## Adding a service

```sh
rizotto service add    # from the repository root, server/ or web/
```

It asks for the service name, whether it works with the database and which CRUD
methods it needs, writes `server/services/<name>/`, registers the queries in `sqlc.yaml`
and prints the lines to add to `server/server.go`. Afterwards run `task gen` to regenerate
`bind-gen.go` (it needs the `genbind` binary:
`go install {{.RizottoModule}}/tools/genbind@latest`) and `task migrate-up` to create the table.

## Adding an HTTP controller

```sh
rizotto controller add
```

It asks which service the controller exposes, which routes it serves, whether they
need an authenticated user and under which prefix they live, then writes
`server/api/<name>-api/` with the controller and its route tests and prints the route table
to join in `server/server.go`. The controller builds its context with `PrivateArea` or
`PublicArea` from `server/api/controller.go`; `task test` runs the route tests, which write
`server/api/<name>-api/openapi.yml` from the document in `server/api/doc`.

## Database

Every service that owns tables keeps them in `server/services/<name>/repository`:

    migrations/   dbmate migrations, the history of the schema, applied in order
    sql/          schema.sql, the shape sqlc checks the queries against, and the queries
    db/           generated by sqlc from sql/ — never edit it

`task gen` regenerates `db/` after a change to a query or to `schema.sql`.

Each service has its own migration history in the table
`public.dbmate_migrations_<name>`, so services never share migration state.
`server/scripts/migrate.sh` walks the services and runs
[dbmate](https://github.com/amacneil/dbmate) for each one; the tasks below run it in
docker against the local PostgreSQL of `server/docker-compose.yml`:

```sh
task db:start                          # PostgreSQL on :5432, the DATABASE_URL of server/.env
task migrate-new -- item add_price     # server/services/item/repository/migrations/<timestamp>_add_price.sql
task migrate-up                        # apply the pending migrations of every service
task migrate-redo -- item              # roll back the last migration of item and apply it again
task migrate-down -- item              # roll back the last migration of item
task migrate-status                    # what is applied and what is pending
task db:reset                          # delete the local database with its data
```

A real environment is never migrated from a workstation: the deploy runs the
`{{.Slug}}-migrations` image (`task docker:build:migrations`) to completion before the
new server starts:

```sh
docker run --rm -e DATABASE_URL=postgres://... {{.Slug}}-migrations
```

Before writing a migration read `.agents/skills/database/SKILL.md`.

## Web

`web/` is a Vite + React + TypeScript app. `task web:dev` serves it on :5173 and
proxies `/api` to the server on :9090, so in development the app and the API share
one origin and the session cookie just works. `task web:build` writes the static
build to `web/dist`.

## Docker

`task docker:build` builds the image `{{.Slug}}` from `.docker/Dockerfile`: the web
app is built with node, the server with go, and both land in one distroless image.
There the server reads `.docker/env` as its `.env`, which sets `STATIC_DIR`, so it
serves the web app from `/` next to the API: a page of the app the browser asks
for gets `index.html`, while a missing asset or API route stays 404.

```sh
docker run --rm -p 9090:9090 -e DATABASE_URL=postgres://... {{.Slug}}
```

Any variable of `server/.env.example` can be overridden with `-e`. To try the same
setup without docker, build the app and start the server with
`STATIC_DIR=../web/dist`.
